BEGIN_TEXT
How the radio side of the box is doing, in one place.

Clock: what set the time. WWV is best: the box listens to WWV and sets
its clock from it; a reading older than 8 hours is shown as stale. GPS is
next, when a GPS receiver is fitted. "Not set" means times come from the
computer's own clock and may be off.

Health check: the box checks itself every few minutes and lists any
problem it finds. The engine is the decoder: it should be connected to
the radio. The scanner log shows whether the scanner is running, whether
it decodes on the engine or here, and how often the radio was overloaded
(a strong signal nearby) or had to restart.

Radio: when the box finds no radio, it says why here.

The data store: everything the box hears is kept on its own data drive
first. If that drive isn't mounted, every screen says so, and what they
show is not what the box has stored.
